By Lemma \ref{lem:prop12thenlengthk}, there is some element $g\in M$ with a unique word representative $w$. We will produce an element $g'\in M$ such that $g$ and $g'$ map to the same quasipositive braid under the natural map $M\to QP_n$. 

  Let $\al,\beta\in\cA_n$ be any pair of arcs. In $B_n$, we have the relation
  \begin{equation*}
    \tau_{\al} \tau_{\beta} \tau_{\al}^{-1}= \tau_{\ga},
  \end{equation*}
  where $\ga=\tau_\al(\beta)$. We can rewrite this as a relation in $QP_n$.
  \begin{equation*}
    \tau_{\al} \tau_{\beta}=\tau_{\ga}\tau_{\al}.
  \end{equation*}
  Let $w'$ be obtained from $w$ by applying this relation to a pair of adjacent generators in $w$. Let $g'=[w']\in M$. Then $g$ and $g'$ are distinct elements of $M$ that map to the same quasipositive braid in $QP_n$.

Recall that we already observed that $\psi$ is an injective homomorphism. We will complete the proof by showing that $\psi$ is surjective. Let $\al \in \cA_n$. We will show that $x_\al\in \psi(B_n)$. We proceed by induction on $N=C(\al)$. 

  The base case is $N=0$. Since $C(\al)=0$, we must have $\al=\al_i$ for some $i$. By definition of $\psi$, we have $x_{\al}\in\psi(B_n)$. 

  Having completed the base case, we proceed with the inductive step. Suppose $x_{\al'}\in\psi(B_n)$ whenever $C(\al')<N$. We will show that $\al$ forms a triangle with no interior punctures with two arcs of smaller complexity. By the above observation about the triangle relations, this will complete the proof.

  Since $C(\al)=N$, there is some $g$ with $|g|=N$ and some $i$ such that
  \begin{equation*}
    \al=g(\al_i).
  \end{equation*}
  Since $|g|=N$, there is some $h$ with $|h|=N-1$ such that 
  \begin{equation*}
    g=h\si_j^{\ep},
  \end{equation*}
  where $\ep=\pm 1$. Moreover, we have $|i-j|=1$ since otherwise we would have 
  \begin{equation*}
    \si_j^{\ep}(\al_i)=\al_i,
  \end{equation*}
  and this would imply that $C(\al)<N$. 

  When $|i-j|=1$, the arcs $\al_i$, $\al_j$, and $\si_j^{\ep}(\al_i)$ form a triangle with no interior punctures. Applying $h$, we see that $\beta=h(\al_i)$, $\ga=h(\al_j)$, and $\al=g(\al_i)$ form a triangle with no interior punctures. Since $|h|=N-1$, we have that $C(\beta)$ and $C(\ga)$ are less than $N$. We have therefore proven that $\al$ forms a triangle with no interior punctures with two arcs of smaller complexity. This completes the proof.

Since $g$ is a common right multiple of $s$ and $t$ and $E$ is closed under right-comultiples, there is some $r \in E$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
      s, t \leq r \leq g.
    \end{equation*}
    Since $s$ and $t$ are distinct, we cannot have $\ell(r)=d$. Otherwise, we would have $s=t=r$. Therefore we must have $\ell(r)=d+1$ and $r=g$. Since $r\in E$, this proves $g \in E$.

Let $E \subset QP_n$ be a Garside family. Let $g \in QP_n$. We will show by induction on its length $\ell(g)$ that $g\in E$, and this will complete the proof. The base cases are lengths 0 and ~1. Suppose $g\in QP_n$ with $\ell(g)=0$. Then $g$ must be the identity, which lies in $E$ due to item (a) of the characterization. 

The next base case is $\ell(g)=1$. Then $g=\tau_{\al}$ for some $\al\in\cA_n$. Since $E$ generates $QP_n$ by item (b), there are elements $s_1,\dotsc, s_r\in E$ such that
\begin{equation*}
  \tau_\al = s_1\dots s_r.
\end{equation*}
Applying the length homomorphism we obtain
\begin{equation*}
  1=\ell(s_1)+\dots + \ell(s_r).
\end{equation*}
Since $\ell$ is nonnegative, we have $\ell(s_i)=1$ for some $i$ and $\ell(s_j)=0$ for $j\neq i$. Therefore we have
\begin{equation*}
  \tau_{\al}=s_i.
\end{equation*}
Since $s_i\in E$, this proves $\tau_{\al}\in E$.

Next we carry out the inductive step. Let $g\in QP_n$ have length $k \geq 2$. Assume all length $k-1$ elements of $QP_n$ lie in $E$. We will prove $g \in E$.

There are two cases, one of which must be proven before the other. The first case is that there are distinct $x,y \in \cH_n$ such that
\begin{equation*}
  g = u x y v
\end{equation*}
with $u,v \in QP_n$. The second case is that $g=x^k$ for some $x\in\cH_n$. We begin with the former case. Let 
\begin{equation*}
  s= uxv, \qquad t = uyv.
\end{equation*}
Moreover, $s$ and $t$ are distinct by cancellativity. By induction, we have $s,t \in E$. We have
\begin{equation*}
  g=s(v^{-1}yv)=t(v^{-1}y^{-1}xyv).
\end{equation*}
Since $v^{-1}yv$ and $v^{-1}y^{-1}xyv$ are positive half-twists, $g$ has length one larger than $s$ and $t$. Since $g$ is also a common right multiple of $s$ and $t$, we can apply Lemma \ref{lem:right comultiple ind condition} and we obtain $g \in E$, as desired. 

Now we consider the case that $g = x^k$ for some $x \in \cH_n$. There is some arc $\al$ such that $x=\tau_\al$. Choose arcs $\beta$ and $\ga$ such that $\al$, $\beta$, and $\ga$ form a triangle. Let $y=\tau_\beta$ and $z=\tau_\ga$. Then the triangle relation gives
\begin{equation*}
  yx=zy.
\end{equation*}
Now we set
\begin{equation*}
  w=x^{k-1}yx = x^{k-1}zy.
\end{equation*}
Then $w$ has length $k+1$ and is a common right multiple of $x^{k-1}y$ and $x^{k-1}z$, which are distinct and of length $k$. Since $x$, $y$, and $z$ are distinct, the length $k$ elements $x^{k-1}y$ and $x^{k-1}z$ belong to the previous case and therefore lie in $E$. By Lemma \ref{lem:right comultiple ind condition}, we get $w \in E$. Finally, we notice that
\begin{equation*}
  w= \left(x^{k-1}y x^{-(k-1)}\right) x^k.
\end{equation*}
Since $E$ is closed under right divisors and $w \in E$, we have $x^k \in E$, as desired.

By induction, we have proven that $E=QP_n$. This completes the proof.
